findings.description:
The HEREDOC regex is `<<-?\s*(['"]?)(WORD)\1[^
]*
(?:.*?
)*?\s*\2[ 	]*(?=
|$)`. The `[^
]*` after the delimiter consumes the REST OF THE OPENING LINE, and strip_heredocs replaces the whole match with `<<HEREDOC`. So in `cat <<EOF > /root/.bashrc` or `cat <<'EOF' | tee /srv/data/out.txt`, the redirect / pipe target on the heredoc line is removed before the ABS_WORD path scan. The header (1.2.2, DEC-0541) says only heredoc BODIES are stripped because 'written file text is data'; the destination path is not body, it is exactly the path the agent reached. This is a new false negative introduced by this change: before 1.2.2 those words were scanned.
